# ---------------- frame story ----------------
C("sauti","Sauti","Ugrasrava|Ugrasravas|son of Lomaharshana|son of Suta","SOW-tee (OOG-ruh-shruh-vuh)","male","human (Suta bard)",
  "adult; frame-story narrator at Naimisha",
  "Outer frame narrator: recites the Mahabharata to Saunaka and the Naimisha Rishis",
  "Ugrasrava, son of Lomaharshana, a Suta versed in the Puranas, arrives at Saunaka's twelve-year sacrifice in the Naimisha forest (§1, §4). He heard the Bharata recited by Vaisampayana at Janamejaya's snake sacrifice and retells it, first the Paushya, Pauloma and Astika histories (§3–§58), then hands over to Vaisampayana's recitation (§59–§60). Saunaka praises him for speaking 'with proper accent and emphasis... even as thy father' (§16).",
  "humble|eloquent|learned|courteous","warm storytelling|reverent|dramatic|gently explanatory",
  f"A mature Indian bard and professional storyteller, warm resonant baritone, fluent and rhythmic, humble yet assured, {A}","1 4 5 13 16 59")
C("saunaka","Saunaka","Kulapati","SHOW-nuh-kuh","male","sage","elder; host of the Naimisha sacrifice",
  "Patriarch (Kulapati) of the Naimisha sages; questions Sauti",
  "Head of the twelve-year sacrifice in the Naimisha forest (§4). He keeps prompting Sauti with questions: why Chyavana was so named (§5), why Janamejaya held the snake sacrifice and how Astika saved the snakes (§13, §16), Indra's fault (§31), Jaratkaru's name (§40), and the stories Vaisampayana recited (§59).",
  "inquisitive|courteous|patriarchal|devout","curious|appreciative|grave",
  f"An elderly Indian sage presiding over a great sacrifice, courteous patriarchal voice, slightly aged and unhurried, inquisitive, {A}","4 5 13 16 31 40 59")
C("vaisampayana","Vaisampayana","","vy-shum-PAA-yuh-nuh","male","sage","elder; inner-frame narrator",
  "Inner frame narrator: disciple of Vyasa who recites the Bharata to Janamejaya",
  "At the command of Krishna-Dwaipayana (Vyasa), his disciple Vaisampayana recites the Bharata to Janamejaya and the Sadasyas during the snake sacrifice (§60). From §60 to §236 nearly every section opens 'Vaisampayana said', and he answers Janamejaya's questions on genealogy (§62–§95) and the deeds of the Kurus and Pandavas.",
  "wise|patient|authoritative|devoted to his guru","calm|solemn|gently dramatic|didactic",
  f"An elderly Indian sage-reciter, deep calm wise voice, slow deliberate measured cadence, gravitas without coldness, {A}","60 61 62 94 95")
C("janamejaya","Janamejaya","son of Parikshit","juh-nuh-MAY-juh-yuh","male","human (Kuru king)","young king",
  "Kuru king, son of Parikshit; holds the snake sacrifice and is Vaisampayana's listener",
  "Son of Parikshit (§3). When Utanka tells him Takshaka killed his father (§50), he questions his ministers (§49–§50) and holds the snake sacrifice to burn all the serpents (§51–§53). He grants the boy Astika a boon, so the sacrifice ends and Takshaka is spared (§56, §58). He then asks Vaisampayana to recite the history of his ancestors (§60) and keeps interrupting with questions (§62, §64, §68, §94, §107, §115, §225, §231).",
  "proud|vengeful at first|curious|reverent to Brahmanas","grief|wrath|eager curiosity|magnanimity",
  f"A young Indian king, bright confident tenor, eager and imperious, with a quick temper that softens into reverent curiosity, {A}","3 49 50 51 56 58 60")
C("vyasa","Vyasa","Krishna-Dwaipayana|Dwaipayana|Krishna Dwaipayana","VYAA-suh","male","sage","ancient sage; ages across the book",
  "Composer of the Bharata; son of Parasara and Satyavati; fathers Dhritarashtra, Pandu and Vidura",
  "Born on an island in the Yamuna to the maiden Kali (Satyavati) by Parasara (§60, §63, §105). At his mother's request he begets Dhritarashtra, Pandu and Vidura (§105–§106). He divides Gandhari's ball of flesh into a hundred and one parts (§115–§116). He appears to the fugitive Pandavas and sends them to Ekachakra (§158). He explains Draupadi's five husbands to Drupada through the tale of the five Indras (§198–§199). He attends Janamejaya's sacrifice, where Vaisampayana recites at his command (§60).",
  "austere|compassionate|prescient|unworldly","serene|grave|tender to his kin|awe-inspiring",
  f"An ancient Indian rishi, dark weathered resonant voice, slow and oracular, kindly beneath the austerity, {A}","60 105 106 115 158 198 199")
C("naimisha_rishis","Rishis of Naimisha","the Rishis|holy men|the Rishi","RISH-eez","male","chorus (sages)","assembled sages",
  "Chorus of ascetics at Saunaka's sacrifice who ask Sauti for the story",
  "The Rishis of the Naimisha forest ask Sauti to recite the Purana first promulgated by Dwaipayana (§1) and to describe Samanta-panchaka (§2), and receive him at the sacrifice (§4).",
  "devout|curious","eager|reverent",f"A gentle chorus of elderly Indian ascetics speaking together, soft and reverent, {A}","1 2 4","chorus")
# ---------------- Paushya parva (§3) ----------------
C("uttanka","Utanka","Uttanka","oot-TUN-kuh","male","human (Brahmana)","young ascetic student",
  "Disciple of Veda; fetches Paushya's queen's ear-rings and later incites Janamejaya against Takshaka",
  "Veda's disciple, who keeps his preceptor's house unblemished (§3). To fetch an honorarium for his preceptress he begs the queen's ear-rings from king Paushya; he and Paushya curse each other (§3). Takshaka steals the ear-rings, and Utanka pursues him into the Naga world; he praises the Nagas and Indra and gets the ear-rings back (§3). Resolving revenge, he goes to Janamejaya and urges him to avenge Parikshit's death by Takshaka (§3, §50).",
  "devoted|intense|indignant|persistent","fervent|indignant|awed|vengeful",
  f"A young Indian ascetic student, earnest intense tenor, fiery when wronged, rapid and passionate, {A}","3 50")
C("veda","Veda","","VAY-duh","male","sage","teacher (householder)",
  "Disciple of Ayoda-Dhaumya; later Utanka's preceptor",
  "Third disciple of Ayoda-Dhaumya, who serves his teacher patiently like an ox (§3). As a householder-teacher he will not burden his own disciples; he leaves Utanka in charge of his house and accepts the ear-rings honorarium (§3).",
  "patient|gentle|kindly","warm|approving",f"A middle-aged Indian teacher, gentle patient baritone, kindly and plain, {A}","3","single_section_speaker")
C("ayoda_dhaumya","Ayoda-Dhaumya","","AA-yo-duh DHOW-myuh","male","sage","elder preceptor",
  "Preceptor who tests the disciples Aruni, Upamanyu and Veda",
  "The Rishi Ayoda-Dhaumya has three disciples (§3). He tests Aruni with the breached dyke and renames him Uddalaka; he starves Upamanyu until he goes blind and then has him praise the Aswins; Veda he tests with long service (§3).",
  "stern|testing|ultimately benevolent","stern|approving|affectionate",
  f"An elderly Indian preceptor, dry stern voice, terse commands, warming into fatherly praise, {A}","3","single_section_speaker")
C("aruni","Aruni","Uddalaka|Aruni of Panchala","AA-roo-nee","male","human (Brahmana disciple)","young disciple",
  "Disciple of Ayoda-Dhaumya who lies in the breach of a dyke",
  "Sent to stop a breach in a watercourse, Aruni lies down in it himself. When his preceptor calls he rises, so the course opens, and he is renamed Uddalaka (§3). In §122 Pandu cites a great Rishi Uddalaka, father of Swetaketu (identity with Aruni-Uddalaka is traditional, not stated in KMG).",
  "obedient|self-effacing","earnest|dutiful",f"A young Indian disciple, earnest soft tenor, obedient and plain-spoken, {A}","3","single_section_speaker")
C("upamanyu","Upamanyu","","oo-puh-MUN-yoo","male","human (Brahmana disciple)","young disciple",
  "Disciple of Ayoda-Dhaumya, blinded by eating arka leaves and healed by the Aswins",
  "Ayoda-Dhaumya forbids Upamanyu every means of food while he tends cattle. He eats arka leaves, goes blind and falls into a well; he praises the Aswins with Rig-Vedic hymns, but refuses their cake without his preceptor's leave, and is rewarded with sight and golden teeth (§3).",
  "devoted|candid|steadfast","earnest|plaintive|devout",f"A thin young Indian disciple, candid tenor, plaintive in hardship, devout and steady, {A}","3","single_section_speaker")
C("paushya","Paushya","King Paushya","POWSH-yuh","male","human (king)","adult king",
  "King whose queen gives Utanka her ear-rings; he and Utanka curse each other",
  "Utanka begs the queen's ear-rings of him. Paushya serves Utanka food with a hair in it; Utanka curses him with blindness, he curses Utanka with childlessness, and they argue about whose curse can be revoked (§3).",
  "courteous|proud|quick to retort","gracious|offended|stubborn",f"A middle-aged Indian king, courtly baritone, gracious turning sharply defensive, {A}","3","single_section_speaker")
# ---------------- Pauloma parva ----------------
